<title>AutoCor:Autocorrelation in raster images</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>
<body>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
<p>This application allows quantifying the degree of spatial autocorrelation of a raster variable by calculating an indicator, in this case the Moran's index.</p> 
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->

<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
<p>Spatial autocorrelation is a property present in the spatial distribution of a variable if the values at any location are influenced by values at nearby locations (following Tobler's 1st Law of Geography). The concept of closeness will be referred to the autocorrelation threshold, limit distance where autocorrelation is very low or non-existent. This threshold is useful to impose the ideal spatial conditions on the sampling of the variable and certain associated statistical models. Spatial autocorrelation makes it possible to identify spatial patterns other than purely random patterns.</p> 
<p>Moran's index is calculated as:</p> 
<p><img height="125" src="AutoCor.png"/></p>
<p><img height="50" src="AutoCor_2.png"/> is the matrix of weights and is worth 1 in the neighboring cells in the north, south, east and west directions and 0.70711 in the diagonals for the Rock case or 0 for the King case; 0 for non-adjacent cells.</p>   
<p><img height="25" src="AutoCor_3.png"/> is the average.</p>  
